00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.340
Imagine the perfect cattle-grazing pasture.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.340
Blue skies, green grass, shared by everyone.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:20.160
Of course, it can still only support so many
cows.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.230
If the farmers were smart, they’d figure
out how many, and divide that among them.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.369
But that’s a big “if”.

00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:30.150
Because the incentives are all wrong.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:35.399
More likely everyone would act in their own
best interest, bringing more and more cows

00:00:35.399 --> 00:00:39.149
until they destroyed the land and no-one could
use it.

00:00:39.149 --> 00:00:41.539
This is The Tragedy of the Commons.

00:00:41.539 --> 00:00:47.600
When resources are shared but limited, people
will act selfishly and ruin it for everyone.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.489
It happens in public parks, traffic, and fishing.

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:56.379
But sometimes, the opposite happens, and that’s
the story of patents.

00:00:56.379 --> 00:00:58.920
Instead of land, it’s ideas.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.699
And the problem is not that everything is
shared, it’s that nothing is.

00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:06.900
Big companies claim everything they lay their
eyes on,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:13.170
They patent the process of making decisions,
rounding rectangles, even, if you can believe

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.950
it, filing patents.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.680
You name it, someone technically owns it.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:22.600
Maybe you don’t plan on using it, but at
least you can stop your competitors.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:29.280
And what’s a few thousand dollars in filing
fees to a company making 265 billion a year?

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.040
But all this ownership leaves little room
for innovation.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.750
Just imagine how hard it would be to design
a new smartphone today without violating anyone’s

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:39.750
patents.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:40.750
Good.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:41.750
Luck.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:45.980
Even if you could somehow figure out which
ones you needed to license, which is already

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.770
a stretch, it would simply be far too expensive.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:55.241
We think of invention as a sudden flash of
inspiration, but usually big ideas are just

00:01:55.241 --> 00:01:59.170
one improvement on the last, or some new combination.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:03.960
Smartphones, for example, are a bunch of different
ideas put together.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.390
And I mean a bunch.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:10.800
Google’s chief legal officer says they might
be susceptible to as many as a quarter million

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:11.900
patent claims.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.730
That’s a mess.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:18.860
All of this means that patents can be not
just ineffective, but actually counterproductive.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.630
So, what’s the solution?

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:27.819
How can we encourage innovation in a way that,
ya know, actually works?

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:30.069
There are two ways to look at patents.

00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:31.980
First, as a right.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.040
Creating things takes time and effort.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:39.700
If you paint a picture, or write a story,
it belongs to you, so why should inventions

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:41.319
be any different?

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:44.970
Physically or otherwise, No-one wants to be
stolen from.

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:49.510
This is why we use the term “Intellectual
Property”, it’s no different than a house

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:50.659
or a car.

00:02:50.659 --> 00:02:57.209
which I’d argue is kind of true - a patent
can be bought, sold, licensed, or abandoned.

00:02:57.209 --> 00:03:02.940
But if ideas are simply property, the inventor
of a life-saving drug could just decide not

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:03.940
to share it.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:09.159
And if companies deserve monopolies over their
creations, we’re stuck with the system we

00:03:09.159 --> 00:03:10.159
have.

00:03:10.159 --> 00:03:16.580
Sure, in theory, we could adjust it, Shorten
the length of patents from 20 to 10, or 5,

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:23.299
or even 2 years, Ban Patent Trolls, Or be
more selective about which patents are approved.

00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:25.939
All of these would be a huge help.

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:28.769
But they wouldn’t solve the fundamental
problem.

00:03:28.769 --> 00:03:32.989
To do that, we need to view them not as a
right but a trade.

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:38.330
You and I promise to give companies a temporary
monopoly, and they invest the time and money

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:40.610
to make the products we want.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:42.310
But we’re being ripped off.

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:47.090
We’re often giving a monopoly when a company
would’ve made their product anyway.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:51.630
In other words, we’re trading a lot for
really nothing in return.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:55.459
Would Apple stop making iPhones if they couldn’t
file patents?

00:03:55.459 --> 00:04:00.079
They might make less money, but they’d almost
certainly still be profitable.

00:04:00.079 --> 00:04:04.800
Even with copycats, they could stand out with
things like better engineering talent and

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:05.800
marketing.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:07.080
How do we know this?

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.750
Because it’s already true.

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:14.920
Xiaomi makes every attempt to replicate products
like the iPhone, but I’d say Apple is still

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.100
doing okay.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:21.890
Or, look at Lego, whose patents expired in
twenty eleven, but still makes billions of

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:22.890
dollars.

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:28.130
If anything losing them made the company more
innovative by giving it better competition.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:30.330
Of course, this isn’t always true.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:35.640
A pharmaceutical company, for example, may
truly need patents to protect its investment.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:40.500
But the goal is to give them just enough incentive,
and not a dollar more.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.490
And that exact amount varies by industry.

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:47.130
Which brings us to the wonderful world of
software patents.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:50.540
From the outside, you may not think it's any
different.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:56.210
An invention is an invention, whether it’s
made of gears or ones and zeros.

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:57.610
But you’d be wrong.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:00.970
With software, the problems are ten times
worse.

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:07.170
They account for about 75% of all troll lawsuits,
and are twice as likely to be litigated.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:08.750
Here’s the difference.

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:12.850
A physical invention can easily be described
as its parts.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:18.310
A ballpoint pen, for example, can be patented
as a tube, an ink reservoir, and a rotating

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:19.310
ball.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:22.070
But a digital one, of course, is made of code.

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:26.080
And because there are many ways to program
the same function, it wouldn’t make sense

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.780
to patent specific lines of code.

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:31.030
Someone could just slightly change it.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:34.050
And, besides, that's already protected by
copyright.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:40.550
So, instead, software patents usually describe
less a process or method and more an idea

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:41.590
or function.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:47.300
That’s the difference between patenting
toy bricks which stack using cylindrical grooves,

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.910
and the idea of connecting two objects.

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:56.300
The later sounds ridiculous, no-one should
own such a basic concept, but it happens all

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.030
the time in software.

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:03.350
The other difference is that, unlike, say,
pharmaceuticals, software development is dirt

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:04.470
cheap.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:08.350
Because nothing is manufactured, there’s
no limit to its scale.

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:14.650
When Instagram was bought by Facebook for
a billion dollars, it employed only 13 people.

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:18.800
And WhatsApp had 55 when it was bought for
20 billion.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:20.490
What matters is speed.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:24.970
A brand new software startup can beat the
biggest companies on the planet if it moves

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:26.130
fast enough.

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:30.770
which means, by the time a patent is granted,
it may no longer be relevant.

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:34.880
Compare this with government, whose strength
is being slow and methodical, and you can

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:37.570
see why it doesn’t mix so well with tech.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:43.180
So, if patents are really a trade, and different
industries require different incentives, it

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:46.090
only makes sense to negotiate individually.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:50.650
Why should the inventor of a buy-now button
be granted just as long a monopoly as the

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:54.250
creator of a drug that took years and years
of research.

00:06:54.250 --> 00:07:00.620
The solution is a prize system, where the
first inventor gets a check, not a monopoly.

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:05.940
This way, companies compete, lower prices,
and make their products more accessible.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:09.870
Not in 20 years, when their patent expires,
but today.

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:13.120
Many new companies would finally have a fighting
chance.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:18.410
And, as a nice cherry on top, patent trolls
would be eliminated overnight.

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:22.530
Of course, there are some challenges, We’d
need a system for determining what kind of

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:24.870
inventions require a prize.

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:27.620
And that money would have to come from somewhere.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:30.910
Which probably means, your favorite, higher
taxes.

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:36.590
Again, it’s not perfect, but it need only
be 1% better than what we have.

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:38.880
And that’s already a pretty low bar.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.300
So, if such a simple solution exists, why
aren't we using it?

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:47.540
For one, because the companies that benefit
most from patents are the ones with money

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:49.270
to lobby for them.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:52.870
And because many people just don't know much
about economics.

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:55.090
There just isn’t much awareness.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:59.340
But patents and the stock market are really
economic games.

